<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarnOn>3390;$(WarnOn)</WarnOn>
    <!-- 0.1.0 (2026-08-06): the first version the three projects are packed and consumed
         at, rather than referenced by project. 0.x pre-1.0 posture: the public surface may
         still move, and a change to it advances this number IN THE SAME COMMIT — the feed
         keys packages by id+version, so repacking an unchanged version swaps the contract
         under every pinned consumer while their extracted caches keep serving the old bits.
         The three package ids version together: they are one contract cut three ways (the
         manager, its dialect seam, its execution port), and the seam references the
         execution tier for the single minting implementation, so a split version would only
         ever be a lie about which pair was tested against which. See VERSIONING.md.

         0.2.0 (2026-08-06): sibling forests and the cross-sibling invariant seam. The
         public surface widened in three ways a consumer feels: `ExplorationOp` gained a
         `Declare` case (an exhaustive match over the op vocabulary stops compiling until it
         handles it), `SpecState` gained `Divergences` (a record construction stops compiling
         until it supplies it), and the dialect seam moved to 1.1 with an OPTIONAL seventh
         obligation. The seam's own version is the separate question VERSIONING.md
         describes: 1.1 is minor because the new obligation is a separate interface a 1.0
         dialect declines by simply not implementing it, so no existing dialect breaks.
         The manager-tier widenings are the reason this is a minor package bump and not a
         patch.

         0.3.0 (2026-08-06): the vintage evidence axis. The public surface widened in four
         ways a consumer feels immediately: `ExplorationOp` gained a `Replay` case and
         `Rejection` gained two (`DuplicateReplay`, `IncompleteReplay`), so an exhaustive
         match over either stops compiling until it handles them; `Exploration` gained
         `Replays` and `Forest` gained `Thresholds`, so a record construction stops compiling
         until it supplies them. `BackendRefusal` and `Rejection` also MOVED file, from
         `Backend.fs` / `Ops.fs` into `Types.fs` — same names, same namespace, no consumer
         difference, but recorded here because a reader diffing the tree will see it. The
         seam tag is unchanged at 1.1: nothing in this release asks a dialect for anything
         new. Both wire additions are byte-additive and pinned as such.

         0.3.1 (2026-08-06): the `Session.ofJsonl` fold-order fix (DECISIONS D34). A PATCH
         bump, and deliberately so: no type gained a case, no record gained a field, no
         signature moved, and no stored byte or node id changes — a history persisted by
         0.3.0 re-folds under 0.3.1 and re-serialises identically. What changes is only the
         order in which stored nodes are folded back, which 0.3.0 decided by a content hash
         and so replayed differently depending on the state ids its author happened to mint.
         Consumers need no source change; they need the repack, because the defect is in the
         bits rather than the surface.

         0.4.0 (2026-08-10): multi-party assent — the second quality axis. A MINOR bump, not
         a patch, and the op vocabulary is why: `ExplorationOp` gained an `Assent` case and
         `OpKind` gained `AssentKind`, so every exhaustive match over either stops compiling
         until it handles them — which is the whole point of a closed algebra and the whole
         cost of extending one. Three more surfaces widen with it: `SpecState` gained
         `Assents`, `Policy` gained `Parties`, and `Capability` gained `MayAssentAs`, so a
         record construction or a capability match stops compiling too. The seam tag is
         unchanged at 1.1: assent asks a dialect for nothing at all — a party class is an
         opaque declared string exactly as an invariant id is, and no obligation was added.

         Both wire additions are byte-additive by construction and pinned as such: a state
         with no assents, and a policy declaring no party class, encode to exactly the bytes
         they did at 0.3.1.

         0.5.0 (2026-08-10): indistinguishability sets — the spec-multiplicity primitive. A
         MINOR bump, and the reason it is minor rather than a patch is the SIZE of the new
         surface rather than any break in the old one: seven public types
         (`IndistinguishabilityScope` / `Declaration` / `Refusal` / `Member` / `Set` /
         `Report` / `Recurrence` / `ForestView`), the `Indistinguishability` module, thirteen
         codec pairs on `Wire`, four reads on `Vintage` (`metrics`, `readingOn`,
         `latestVintage`, `baselineVintage`), `Query.outcomesOn`, and two more laws in
         `Contract.laws`. `Vintage.wereAlternatives` also moved from private to public,
         deliberately: the new surface is restricted by exactly that rule and must reach it
         through the same function rather than a second copy, since two copies of a discipline
         are two disciplines the moment either is edited.

         Nothing existing changed shape. No type gained a case, no record gained a field, no
         signature moved, and the op vocabulary is untouched — this release adds no op, because
         a derived read over the folded record is not an act and does not belong in a history.
         So an exhaustive match written against 0.4.0 still compiles, which is precisely what
         makes this additive rather than breaking. The seam tag is unchanged at 1.1:
         indistinguishability asks a dialect for nothing at all — a metric name is an opaque
         declared string exactly as an invariant id is.

         The wire additions are new documents rather than new fields on existing ones, so
         nothing already stored re-encodes differently; the byte pins on the declaration and on
         the no-declaration report state the new shapes as facts rather than intentions.

         0.5.1 (2026-08-21): the Fuaran.Core pins raised 0.3.0 -> 0.6.0 — the cross-platform
         FNV-1a parity release (fuaran-core#105's split-half mul32 multiply). A PATCH bump on
         the 0.3.1 precedent exactly: no type gained a case, no record gained a field, no
         signature moved, and no stored byte or node id changes — .NET-minted hash values are
         unchanged, so a history persisted by 0.5.0 re-folds and re-verifies identically. What
         changes is the packed dependency floor: a consumer restoring 0.5.0 resolves a Core
         whose chain hash diverges under any JavaScript lowering, which made the manager's
         cross-host-replay guarantee false for a JS host. Consumers need no source change;
         they need the repack, because the defect was in the substrate's bits rather than in
         this surface.

         0.7.0 (2026-10-13): a diagnostic states its unit (DECISIONS D39). A MINOR bump, and
         the record fields are why: `OutcomeSnapshot` gained `DiagnosticUnits`, `GateRequest`
         and `GateVerdict` gained `Unit`, and on the execution tier the mirror's `FitOutcome`,
         `Gate`, `GateVerdict` and `ReferenceExecutorConfig` gained the same — so every
         full-literal construction stops compiling until it supplies them (FS0764). Three
         closed unions widened with them, so an exhaustive match stops compiling too:
         `BackendRefusal` gained `GateUnitMismatch`, the mirror's `Refusal` gained the
         specification's new `gateUnitMismatch` class, and `Rejection` gained
         `HistoryCollision`. `Bridge.toManagerVerdict` and `toManagerOutcome` now return a
         `Result`, because a unit that is not unit text is refused rather than dropped.
         0.6.0 is tagged, so this advances rather than riding it.

         The Fuaran.Core pins moved 0.20.0 -> 0.37.0 in the same release, because
         `Fuaran.Core.Unit` first ships at 0.37.0 and the family moves as one version. Every
         wire byte the manager wrote at 0.6.0 is unchanged — an outcome, gate or policy that
         states no unit encodes to exactly the bytes it did, which the contract pack's byte
         pins assert. The seam tag is unchanged at 1.1: a unit is a value attached to a name
         a dialect already chose, and nothing new is asked of a dialect.

         0.7.0 was untagged and no published consumer pinned it, so it was still a draft, and the
         Fuaran.Core pins moving 0.37.0 -> 0.41.0 (DECISIONS D41) ride it rather than advance it:
         no type gained a case, no record gained a field, no signature moved and no stored byte
         changes. What moved is the packed dependency floor, which the first tagged release states
         once. -->
    <Version>0.7.0</Version>
    <!-- Every package this repository depends on restores from nuget.org (nuget.config), so a pin
         must name a tagged, published version. -->
    <FuaranRestorePath>public</FuaranRestorePath>
    <Authors>Andrew J. Willshire</Authors>
    <Company>Diametrical Ltd</Company>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <Copyright>Copyright 2026 Diametrical Ltd</Copyright>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Fuaran-Data/Fuaran-Modelling</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Fuaran-Data/Fuaran-Modelling</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
